\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The unproved tuning and preservation hypotheses}
\date{2026-11-28}

\p{\ref{fdip-01DO} assumes that one initial mass
#{\nu_0} produces an exact orbit satisfying all the
[controlled input bounds](fdip-01DK) for #{n<K}. Existence of
that mass and preservation of the derivative norm and both
curvature bounds are not proved. The one-step output estimate
controls a weaker function norm and supplies none of those
three input bounds at the next step.}

\p{\ref{fdip-01DP} gives potentials with small
Gaussian remainders and the prescribed quartic growth far from
the origin. It does not show that these potentials are consecutive
members of an exact orbit. A continuity argument for the initial
mass would additionally need endpoint signs and estimates that
hold uniformly through the chosen depth. The conditional result
therefore constructs neither an unconditional interacting limit
nor a non-Gaussian fixed point.}
